---
import Site from "../layouts/Site.astro";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<Site
  title="Learn with microcontrollers"
  description="Explore electronics, computing and communications together. Small experiments with lights and microcontrollers help you understand, imagine and make."
>
  <section class="wrap page-intro narrow">
    <p class="eyebrow">PLAY · INVESTIGATE · MAKE</p>
    <h1>Learn how the whole thing works.</h1>
    <p class="intro">
      A microcontroller brings a circuit, a program and a conversation between
      devices together.
    </p>
    <p>
      Making a light change colour gives you three things to investigate: the
      electronics that make it possible, the computation that decides what
      happens, and the signals that carry instructions. Learning a little of
      each opens up much more than learning a programming language alone.
    </p>
    <p>
      These are suggested routes, not a required order. Start with play, a
      question or something you want to make. The browser lessons are available
      now; the electronics and communications activities below are early
      investigation prompts, not a classroom-tested course.
    </p>
    <nav class="guide-links" aria-label="Learning areas">
      <a href="#electronics">Electronics</a>
      <a href="#computing">Computing</a>
      <a href="#communications">Communications</a>
      <a href="#your-idea">Your own idea</a>
    </nav>
  </section>
  <section class="wrap section narrow prose" id="electronics">
    <p class="eyebrow">01 / ENERGY AND SIGNALS</p>
    <h2>Electronics: follow the circuit.</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>First, make a light glow.</strong> Follow the{" "}
      <a href={`${base}build/`}>quick Pico build</a> with an adult helper, or
      try the lights on screen. Change the colour. What would you like it to do
      next?
    </p>
    <ul class="parts-list">
      <li>
        Invent a colour signal for “come in”, “busy” or “snack time”. Can
        someone else guess your message?
      </li>
      <li>
        Unplug both supplies and trace the wires with your finger. Which ones
        bring power? Which one carries the colour instructions?
      </li>
      <li>
        Compare the <a href={`${base}build/#connections`}>quick circuit</a> with
        the <a href={`${base}build/#make-it-reliable`}>upgraded one</a>. What
        has been added? Pick one part and find out which problem it helps solve.
      </li>
      <li>
        Something flashing when it should stay still? Choose one test from the{" "}
        <a href={`${base}build/#glitches`}>glitch detective</a>. Make a
        prediction, test it with your helper, and compare what happened.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      You are finding out how power and information work together. The quick
      Pico route has no data resistor or level shifter. Once it glows, you can
      explore{" "}
      <a href={`${base}build/#why-direct-works`}>
        why it works and when extra parts help
      </a>
      .
    </p>
    <p>
      Disconnect both supplies before changing wiring. Follow the build guide
      for the first powered experiment.
    </p>
    <a class="text-link" href={`${base}build/`}>
      Make your first light glow →
    </a>
  </section>
  <section class="wrap section narrow prose" id="computing">
    <p class="eyebrow">02 / INSTRUCTIONS, MEMORY AND TIME</p>
    <h2>Computing: explain the behaviour.</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Start with:</strong> the{" "}
      <a href="https://kajlights.github.io/lightvm/">lighting playground</a>.
      Choose a lesson under <strong>Learn with lights</strong>, then use{" "}
      <strong>Try it here → Start preview</strong>. No board is needed. Python,
      JavaScript and visual blocks offer different ways to express the same
      ideas.
    </p>
    <ul class="parts-list">
      <li>
        Predict what an example will do before running it. Change one colour or
        timing value, then compare the result with your prediction.
      </li>
      <li>
        Follow a loop across the pixels. How does the program know which light
        it is changing?
      </li>
      <li>
        Use the lesson’s stepping controls to investigate what changes between
        frames. What must the program remember to make something move?
      </li>
      <li>
        Save a pattern and run it on a board. Distinguish editable source, the
        compiled program and the firmware that executes it.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <strong>What you are exploring:</strong> representation, logic, state,
      memory, timing and debugging. Coding is one tool for expressing these
      ideas. Predicting, drawing a sequence or explaining an unexpected result
      are useful ways to work with them too.
    </p>
    <p>
      The twelve existing beginner lessons move from instructions, names and
      repetition to choices, time, functions, arrays and small installations. A
      simulated light helps you study a rule; real hardware also introduces
      power, wiring and timing constraints.
    </p>
    <a class="text-link" href="https://kajlights.github.io/lightvm/">
      Open the lessons and preview ↗
    </a>
  </section>
  <section class="wrap section narrow prose" id="communications">
    <p class="eyebrow">03 / MESSAGES BETWEEN THINGS</p>
    <h2>Networking & communications: follow a message.</h2>
    <p>
      A data signal to an LED, a USB command from a browser and a message over
      Wi-Fi all carry information. They use different connections and rules. A
      protocol is an agreement about how to represent a message and what it
      means.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Start with:</strong> an existing LightVM device and{" "}
      <a href={`${base}studio/#/devices`}>My devices</a>. Read its identity and
      features. Give it a nickname and ask why a friendly name is different from
      the identity used to send a command to the right board.
    </p>
    <ul class="parts-list">
      <li>
        Draw the path from your browser to the board and from the board to the
        LEDs. Where is the program running? Where is a message being sent?
      </li>
      <li>
        With a Wi-Fi-enabled board and firmware, compare USB with a
        local-network connection. What are the jobs of an address, a device name
        and discovery?
      </li>
      <li>
        Run an animation on the device, then close the browser while leaving the
        board powered. Predict whether the lights will stop, and explain what
        you observe.
      </li>
      <li>
        For a later experiment, use MQTT with an existing broker. A publisher
        sends a message on a topic; subscribers receive messages for topics they
        follow. What should a light do if updates stop arriving?
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <strong>What you are exploring:</strong> addressing, protocols,
      publish/subscribe, independence and recovery. Network activities need the
      relevant firmware features and a local network; MQTT also needs a broker.
      The original Pico has no Wi-Fi. These are optional extensions to the first
      USB lighting project, and currently use developer-level guides.
    </p>
    <div class="guide-links">
      <a href="https://github.com/KajLights/lightvm/blob/main/docs/networking.md">
        Network setup and concepts ↗
      </a>
      <a href="https://github.com/KajLights/lightvm/blob/main/docs/mqtt.md">
        MQTT and Home Assistant ↗
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="wrap section narrow prose" id="your-idea">
    <p class="eyebrow">UNDERSTANDING → IMAGINATION → MAKING</p>
    <h2>Use what you know to ask for something new.</h2>
    <p>
      Imagine a light that signals a message, responds to a sensor or shows the
      state of a room. Sketch the input, the decision and the output. Identify
      which parts you have, what they can do and what you still need to find
      out. Those ideas are starting points to design, not promises that every
      sensor or connection is already supported here.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you use an AI assistant, bring it that context. Ask it to explain its
      assumptions and propose a small test. For example:
    </p>
    <blockquote class="learning-prompt">
      I have a Pico 2 W running LightVM and 12 RGB LEDs on GP16. I want a moving
      colour pattern. Explain what must happen in the circuit, the program and
      the LED data signal. What can I test in the browser first, and what needs
      checking on the real board?
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      Compare the answer with the actual parts, documentation and observations.
      Ask what happens at startup, when a message is late or when power is
      removed. Understanding gives you more to imagine, a clearer request to
      make and a better way to judge what is built.
    </p>
    <p>
      For a group or classroom, invite people to predict, draw, build and
      explain in different roles. Adapt the activities to their experience; this
      project’s educational aims still need evaluation in practice.
    </p>
    <a class="text-link" href={`${base}about/#why-understanding`}>
      Read about the shared aim with Found in Space →
    </a>
  </section>
</Site>
